Race condition from read-modify-write pattern. Two requests read same value before either writes back.

**Wrong (causes race):**
```js
const { rows } = await db.query('SELECT count FROM counters WHERE id = $1', [id]);
const newCount = rows[0].count + 1;
await db.query('UPDATE counters SET count = $1 WHERE id = $2', [newCount, id]);
```

**Fix — atomic update, return new value:**
```js
const { rows } = await db.query(
  'UPDATE counters SET count = count + 1 WHERE id = $1 RETURNING count',
  [id]
);
return rows[0].count;
```

Single statement. Postgres handles atomicity. No race possible.

---

If counter row might not exist yet, use upsert:
```js
const { rows } = await db.query(`
  INSERT INTO counters (id, count) VALUES ($1, 1)
  ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET count = counters.count + 1
  RETURNING count
`, [id]);
return rows[0].count;
```

Root cause: never read then write in separate queries for derived values. Push arithmetic into SQL.
